WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.779
Have you ever got stuck in a video game?

00:00:07.779 --> 00:00:11.289
Or found a glitch that would prevent you from
finishing it?

00:00:11.289 --> 00:00:17.500
As many of you know, most well-known computer
games undergo a ton of playtesting, an important

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.050
step that is supposed to unveil these issues.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:27.550
So how is it possible that all these bugs
and glitches still make it the final product?

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.870
Why did the creators not find these issues?

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:35.510
Well, you see, playtesting is often done by
humans.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:38.829
That sounds like a good thing, and it often
is.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:44.290
But, here comes the problem - whenever we
change something in the game, our changes

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:50.590
may also have unintended consequences somewhere
else away from where we applied them.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:56.300
New oversights may appear elsewhere, for instance,
moving a platform may make the level more

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:01.299
playable, however, also, this might happen.

00:01:01.299 --> 00:01:06.720
The player may now be able to enter a part
of the level that shouldn’t be accessible,

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.500
or, be more likely to encounter a collision
bug and get stuck.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.770
Unfortunately, all this means that it’s
not enough to just test what we have changed,

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:24.210
but we have to retest the whole level, or
maybe the whole game itself.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:28.110
For every single change, no matter how small.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:34.500
That not only takes a ton of time and effort,
but is often flat out impractical.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.740
So what is the solution?

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:42.810
Apparently, a proper solution would require
asking tons of curious humans to test the

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:43.810
game.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.860
But wait a second.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:49.630
We already have curious learning-based algorithms.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.250
Can we use them for playtesting?

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.730
That sounds amazing!

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:57.090
Well, yes, until we try it.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:03.980
You see, here is an automated agent, but a
naive one trying to explore the level.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.409
Unfortunately, it seems to have missed half
the map!

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:11.240
Well, that’s not the rigorous testing we
are looking for, is it?

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.200
Let’s see what this new AI offers.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.780
Can it do any better?

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.590
Oh my, now we’re talking!

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:28.660
The new technique was able to explore not
less than 50%, but a whopping 95% of the map.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:29.670
Excellent.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:36.000
But we are experienced Fellow Scholars over
here, so of course, we have some questions.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.810
So, apparently this one has great coverage,
so it can cruise around, great, but our question

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:47.390
is, can these AI agents really find game-breaking
issues?

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:53.840
Well, look, it just found a bug where it could
climb to the top of the platform without having

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.100
to use the elevator.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:03.490
It can also build a graph that describes which
parts of the level are accessible and through

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.319
what path.

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:06.810
Look!

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:11.700
This visualization tells us about the earlier
issue where one could climb the wall through

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:17.950
an unintentional issue, and, after the level
designer supposedly fixed it by adjusting

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:24.240
the steepness of the wall, let’s see the
new path.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.290
Yes, now it could only get up there by using
the elevator.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:32.720
That is the intended way to traverse the level.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.130
Excellent!

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:39.200
And it gets better, it can even tell us the
trajectories that enabled it to leave the

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.720
map so we know exactly what issues we need
to fix without having to look through hours

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.690
and hours of video footage.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:52.480
And, whenever we applied the fixes, we can
easily unleash another bunch of these AIs

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:58.530
to search every nook and cranny, and try these
crazy strategies, even ones that don’t make

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:02.240
any sense, but appear to work well.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.240
So, how long does this take?

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:11.959
Well, the new method can explore half the
map in approximately an hour or two, can explore

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:19.570
90% of the map in about 28 hours, and if we
give it a couple more days, it goes up to

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.959
about 95%.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:27.010
That is quite a bit, so we don’t get immediate
feedback as soon as we change something, since

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:32.280
this method is geared towards curiosity and
not efficiency.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.530
Note that this is just the first crack at
the problem, and I would not be surprised

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:42.030
if just one more paper down the line, this
would take about an hour, and two more papers

00:04:42.030 --> 00:05:01.690
down the line, it might even be done in a
matter of minutes.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:51.300
What a time 
to be alive!

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.930
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

